%description:

Protocol Test Framework self test -- the nextTimes sequencing word.

`nextTimes(n, ...)` matches the next n events and then lets the steps after it begin. It
**sequences**; it does not bound. An n+1th match is not forbidden, because by then the step
has finished and the step after it owns the stream.

That is the difference from `exactlyTimes(n, ...)`, which is a cardinality: it waits its
window out, fails at once on an n+1th match, and fails on too few when the window closes.
`self/Repeat.test` covers that one.

The two used to share the name `exactlyTimes`, and the cardinality was the one that did not
exist. A check that said "exactly three" got "the next three" and passed over everything
after them.

host1 sends a datagram every 0.2 s from 0.1 s. This test takes the first three with
`nextTimes`, and then observes a fourth. The fourth is the proof: after a cardinality of
three the fourth datagram would be a failure, and after a sequence of three it is simply the
next event, which a later step may read.

Expected result: PASS.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("next_times")
        // The next three datagrams. The step resolves on the third.
        .nextTimes(3, on("host1.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("udp.destPort == 5000")
                  .describe("the next three datagrams to port 5000")
                  .within(2.0))
        // And the fourth, which the step above neither consumed nor forbade. A cardinality
        // of three would have failed on this event; a sequence of three hands it on.
        .once(on("host1.udp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("udp.destPort == 5000")
                  .describe("the fourth datagram, which the sequence left for this step")
                  .within(2.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "next_times compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// Two hosts on one link -- note: NO tester (attached at runtime).
network NextTimesNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = NextTimesNet
sim-time-limit = 3s
seed-set = 0

**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
*.host1.app[0].destAddresses = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].destPort = 5000
*.host1.app[0].messageLength = 100B
*.host1.app[0].sendInterval = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].startTime = 0.1s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "UdpSink"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 5000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST next_times: PASS
